# REGRESSION — an indirect call through a `Callable[void(int &)]` LOCAL writes
# through to the caller's `int` slot exactly as calling `bump(&a)` directly does.
#
# AXIS / CELL (root x callable-provenance x arg-spelling):
#   plain-local int root  x  LOCAL callable  x  `&`-spelled arg
# This is the reference's canonical `&`-in-a-function-type spelling
# (§9.1 position table), and the LOCAL cell of the callable-indirection axis.
#
# Pre-fix: `gg check` clean, `gg build` clean, binary exits 139 (SIGSEGV) on both
# C and LLVM. Root: `types.rs` mapped `Type::Function { .. }` to `GirType::FnPtr`
# with the `&` dropped from the params axis, and the non-routing indirect-call
# arms in `src/ir/lowering/exprs/calls.rs` never consulted the callee's declared
# param types, so `cb(&a)` handed the callee a VALUE where the body dereferenced
# a pointer.
#
# Fix (Track B1): the callable-typed local now carries `callable_param_types` +
# `callable_param_ownerships` sidecars written at every function-registration
# path and read at both indirect-call arms, which route each user arg through
# `lower_call_arg` on the callee's sig — the standard direct-call arg lowering
# with the pointer-vs-value forwarding pass.
void bump(int &n):
    n = n + 100

void main():
    int a = 1
    Callable[void(int &)] cb = bump
    cb(&a)
    print(a)
